WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.408
Where might this pole break?

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.635
Start with the sideways load at its physical tip.

00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:11.764
Bending demand increases down the pole.

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:15.935
The section on the right shows the timber available to resist it.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.354
The pole also thickens towards the ground.

00:00:21.296 --> 00:00:23.144
Demand is only half the story.

00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:28.924
Here, green and blue show utilisation below the assumed timber strength.

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:35.377
Applied stress, material strength and their ratio are different quantities.

00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:40.510
This pole has a weakened region above ground.

00:00:42.252 --> 00:00:43.706
Look at the remaining timber.

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:46.206
It still carries load.

00:00:47.143 --> 00:00:51.559
A defect can change the governing height; this close view does not identify

00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:51.871
it.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:55.802
The load now points in another direction.

00:00:57.544 --> 00:00:58.563
Follow the arrow.

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:03.495
An off-centre defect can respond differently when bending changes direction.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:06.694
These are beam bending estimates.

00:01:07.632 --> 00:01:11.830
Splitting, shell instability and real fracture need separate assessment.
